{"duration_api_ms":44542,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"dc466bc8-b856-4c6b-8bc5-2f26bac5913d","total_cost_usd":0.20619579999999998,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12002,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"output_tokens":5494,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2722},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12002,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5494,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12002,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12002},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":5494,"cacheReadInputTokens":1459,"cacheCreationInputTokens":12002,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.20619579999999998,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":2722,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# BIP: 39\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nThis BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\\n\\nIt consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed. This seed can later be used to generate deterministic wallets using BIP-0032 or similar methods.\\n\\n## Copyright\\n\\nThis BIP falls under the GPL License.\\n\\n## Motivation\\n\\nA mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed. The sentence could be written on paper or spoken over the telephone.\\n\\nThis guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription. It's not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\\n\\n## Generating the mnemonic\\n\\nThe mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits. With more entropy security is improved but the sentence length increases. We refer to the initial entropy length as ENT. The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\\n\\nFirst, an initial entropy of ENT bits is generated. A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash. This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy. Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist. Finally, we convert these numbers into words and use the joined words as a mnemonic sentence.\\n\\nThe following table describes the relation between the initial entropy length (ENT), the checksum length (CS), and the length of the generated mnemonic sentence (MS) in words.\\n\\n```\\nCS = ENT / 32\\nMS = (ENT + CS) / 11\\n\\n|  ENT  | CS | ENT+CS |  MS  |\\n+-------+----+--------+------+\\n|  128  |  4 |   132  |  12  |\\n|  160  |  5 |   165  |  15  |\\n|  192  |  6 |   198  |  18  |\\n|  224  |  7 |   231  |  21  |\\n|  256  |  8 |   264  |  24  |\\n```\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0039.mediawiki\",\"decisions\":[],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"a3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\"reason\":\"Abstract: corrected 'letters' to 'words' and 'UTOXs' to 'wallets'.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\"reason\":\"Abstract: BIP 39 produces a binary seed, not an ASCII seed.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This seed can later be used to generate deterministic wallets using BIP-0032 or similar methods.\",\"reason\":\"Abstract: word order smoothed.\"},{\"segment\":\"a9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"A mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed.\",\"reason\":\"Motivation: the raw representation is of a wallet seed, not satoshis.\"},{\"segment\":\"a11\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription.\",\"reason\":\"Motivation: what is transported is randomness, not determinism.\"},{\"segment\":\"a12\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"It's not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\",\"reason\":\"Motivation: fixed typo 'süeed'.\"},{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"## Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"Section heading: fixed misspelling 'memonic'.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits.\",\"reason\":\"Fixed 'demonic' typo and corrected 23 to 32 bits.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\",\"reason\":\"ENT range is 128-256 bits, as the table itself shows.\"},{\"segment\":\"b7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\"reason\":\"Hash function is SHA256; 'SHA652' is a typo.\"},{\"segment\":\"b8\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\"reason\":\"Checksum is appended to the end, not the start.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist.\",\"reason\":\"Groups are 11 bits (0-2047), consistent with MS = (ENT + CS) / 11.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\"corrects\":\"a group of easy to remember letters -- for the generation of deterministic UTOXs\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 mnemonics are words and generate deterministic wallets.\"},{\"statement\":\"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\"corrects\":\"converting it into a ASCII seed\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 derives a 512-bit binary seed via PBKDF2.\"},{\"statement\":\"A mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed.\",\"corrects\":\"representations of a wallet satoshis\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"The mnemonic represents a wallet seed, not satoshis.\"},{\"statement\":\"This guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription.\",\"corrects\":\"transport computer-generated determinism\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"The mnemonic encodes random entropy; the BIP says randomness.\"},{\"statement\":\"The mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits.\",\"corrects\":\"a multiple of 23 bits\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"CS = ENT / 32 and the table's ENT values are multiples of 32.\"},{\"statement\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\",\"corrects\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 1024-2048 byts.\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"The document's own table spans ENT 128 to 256 bits.\"},{\"statement\":\"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\"corrects\":\"its SHA652 hash\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 uses SHA256; SHA652 does not exist.\"},{\"statement\":\"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\"corrects\":\"appended to the start of the initial entropy\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 appends the checksum after the entropy bits.\"},{\"statement\":\"Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist.\",\"corrects\":\"split into groups of 12 bits\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"0-2047 needs 11 bits, matching MS = (ENT + CS) / 11.\"}]}","ttft_ms":30924,"type":"result","duration_ms":49142,"uuid":"038988cc-c1fc-4c7a-854a-a40489c434cb","ttft_stream_ms":6889,"time_to_request_ms":4599,"first_content_frame_ms":6889,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
